WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:07.060
You walk alone into a room to find a chessboard.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:11.540
Each of the 64 squares has a coin sitting
on top of it.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:15.200
Backing up, this is going to be one of those
classic prisoner puzzles, where a strangely

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:21.320
math-obsessed warden offers you and a fellow
inmate a chance for freedom, but only if you

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.510
can solve some elaborate scheme they’ve
laid out.

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:28.890
In this case, that warden has carefully turned
over the coins to be heads or tails according

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:34.640
to whatever pattern they want, and they then
show you a key, and put it inside one of the

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.040
chessboard squares [each square is a secret
compartment or something like that].

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:44.180
So you know where the key is, and your goal
is to get your fellow prisoner to also know

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:49.560
where the key is, but the only thing you’re
allowed to do before leaving the room is turn

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.930
over one, and only one, of these coins.

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:58.900
At that point, you walk out, your fellow prisoner
walks in, and with no information other than

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:03.620
the set of heads and tails they’re looking
at, which you’ve barely tweaked, they’re

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:10.980
supposed to deduce where the key is hidden,
potentially winning freedom for both of you.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:15.299
You can strategize ahead of time, but you
won’t know what the specific layout of heads

00:01:15.299 --> 00:01:17.040
and tails will be.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.960
Moreover, the warden can listen in to your
strategy and do their best to thwart it with

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:26.179
some adversarial arrangement of the coins
and the key.

00:01:26.179 --> 00:01:30.460
So I first heard about this puzzle over during
dinner conversation at a wedding actually,

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:32.430
and it just totally sucked me in.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:38.579
I remember the drive home was, maybe, 3 hours,
and I think my mind was on flipping coins

00:01:38.579 --> 00:01:41.180
and encoding states that whole time.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:45.729
But the puzzle sticks with you, even after
solving it, I fell into these two surprisingly

00:01:45.729 --> 00:01:47.509
interesting rabbit holes.

00:01:47.509 --> 00:01:53.180
One was to prove this challenge is impossible
if you vary the setup a little bit, for example

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:56.950
if the chessboard was 6x6, or if you removed
one of its squares.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:02:01.829
To give you some sense for where that rabbit
hole leads, this video will end with an especially

00:02:01.829 --> 00:02:05.869
pleasing way to paint the corners of a 4-dimensional
cube.

00:02:05.869 --> 00:02:09.539
The other rabbit hole was to work out how
closely you could connect the solution of

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:14.659
this puzzle with error-correction, which is
a super important topic in computer science

00:02:14.659 --> 00:02:16.540
and information theory.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:21.650
The idea is that when computers send and store
data, the messiness of the real world inevitably

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:27.239
flips a bit now and then, which completely
changes how that data is read, so error correcting

00:02:27.239 --> 00:02:31.930
codes are a way to add a shockingly small
amount of added information to a message that

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:37.799
makes it possible for a receiver to identify
that there was an error, and more impressively

00:02:37.799 --> 00:02:38.909
how to fix it.

00:02:38.909 --> 00:02:44.290
It turns out the intuitions for solving this
puzzle are basically identical to the intuitions

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:48.040
behind these things called “Hamming Codes”,
which are one of the earliest examples of

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:49.709
highly efficient error correction.

00:02:49.709 --> 00:02:54.230
Which is all to say that time spent mulling
over this problem is not as useless as you

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:55.659
might think.

00:02:55.659 --> 00:03:00.900
We actually won’t talk about the solution
here; instead I filmed a video all about that

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:05.099
over on StandupMaths with Matt Parker, who
many of you may recognize from his combined

00:03:05.099 --> 00:03:07.440
YouTube, stand up and book fame.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.150
We talk about our thought process in solving
it, and it’s good fun because there are

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:13.709
multiple ways of looking at it.

00:03:13.709 --> 00:03:18.830
Instead what I want to do here is take a more
global view of all possible strategies for

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:23.790
this puzzle, and bring you with me down that
first rabbit hole of proving why certain variations

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:29.099
must always leave room for the warden to thwart
you, no matter how clever you are.

00:03:29.099 --> 00:03:33.569
The proof itself involves one of those satisfying
moments where a shift in perspective reveals

00:03:33.569 --> 00:03:38.090
a solution, and the whole context leading
to it is a nice chance to practice reasoning

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:42.709
about higher dimensional objects as a way
to draw conclusions about information and

00:03:42.709 --> 00:03:43.709
data.

00:03:43.709 --> 00:03:48.190
Plus, it’ll do more to help you appreciate
the solution to the original puzzle when you

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:59.840
can see how it is, in a sense, almost impossible.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.300
We want some kind of visualization for what
it even means to solve this puzzle.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:08.480
To build up to the general case, let’s knock
things down to the simplest possible case

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:14.080
we can that still has any kind of meaning
to it: Two squares, two coins, two possibilities

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:17.230
for where the key is.

00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:20.680
One way you could solve this is to simply
let the second coin communicate where the

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:25.500
key is; if it’s tails, it means the key
is in the left square, and if its heads, the

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:27.140
key is in the right square.

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:34.479
So if it needs to be changed, you can flip
it, and if it doesn’t, just flip the other

00:04:34.479 --> 00:04:36.419
coin.

00:04:36.419 --> 00:04:39.840
First thing’s first, let’s stop thinking
of these as heads and tails, and start thinking

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:44.169
about 1’s and 0’s; much easier to do math
with.

00:04:44.169 --> 00:04:49.290
Then you can think of these pairs of coins
as a set of coordinates, where the four possible

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:54.740
states now all sit on the corners of a unit
square.

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:58.410
This might feel like a silly thing to do when
we already know how to solve this case, but

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:01.850
it’s a good warmup for turning the larger
cases into geometry.

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:07.190
Notice, flipping one of the coins moves you
along an edge of this square, since it’s

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:08.190
only changing one coordinate.

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:15.340
The strategy of letting that second coin encode
the key location can be drawn by associating

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:20.880
those bottom two corners, where the y-coordinate
is 0, with the “key is under square 0”

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:28.569
state, and the top two corners with the “key
is under square 1” state.

00:05:28.569 --> 00:05:34.190
Think about what it means for our solution
to work: No matter where you start, if you’re

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:39.569
forced to take a step along an edge, forced
to flip one coin, you can always guarantee

00:05:39.569 --> 00:05:47.039
you end up in whichever of these two regions
you want.

00:05:47.039 --> 00:05:50.380
Now the question is, what does all this look
like for bigger chess boards?

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:54.850
The next simplest case would be three squares,
three coins, three possibilities for where

00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:56.870
the key is.

00:05:56.870 --> 00:06:02.350
This gives 8 possible states the coins could
be in, and playing the same game of interpreting

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:07.800
these states as coordinates, it now brings
us to 3d space, with each state sitting at

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:10.479
the corner of a unit cube.

00:06:10.479 --> 00:06:14.690
The usefulness of a picture like this is that
it gives a very vivid meaning to turning over

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:15.690
one of the coins.

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:23.120
Each flip is a step along one edge of the
cube.

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:29.919
Now, what would it mean for you and your fellow
inmate to have a strategy for this puzzle?

00:06:29.919 --> 00:06:34.090
Whenever prisoner 2 walks into the room, they
need to be able to associate the state they’re

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:38.810
looking at, 3 bits, essentially, with one
of three possible squares.

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:43.039
We’re already thinking very visually, so
let’s think of those three possibilities

00:06:43.039 --> 00:06:48.720
as colors, red for square 0, green for square
1, blue for square 2.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:54.730
In this conception, coming up with a strategy,
any strategy, is the same thing as coloring

00:06:54.730 --> 00:07:02.320
each of the 8 corners of a cube either red,
green or blue.

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:06.680
For example, coloring the whole cube red would
– well, I don’t know if you’d call this

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:10.940
a strategy exactly – it would correspond
to always guessing that the key is in square

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:12.200
0.

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:20.070
If instead you let the sum of the first two
coins encode the key location, the cube would

00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:21.520
look like this.

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:26.500
What’s kind of fun is that we can count
how many total strategies exist.

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:31.949
With three choices for the color of each vertex,
we get 3^8.

00:07:31.949 --> 00:07:36.919
Or, if you’re comfortable letting your mind
stray to the thought of painting a 64-dimensional

00:07:36.919 --> 00:07:45.880
cube, you can think about the sense in which
there are 64^(2^64) total possible strategies.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:50.710
This is the size of the haystack when we’re
looking for a needle.

00:07:50.710 --> 00:08:00.330
Another attempt might look like taking 0*coin0
plus 1*coin1 + 2*coin2, and reduce it mod

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:05.599
3 if needed; over on Stand-up Maths Matt and
I talk about trying a version of this for

00:08:05.599 --> 00:08:10.009
the 64 square case, why it works decently
well for a random arrangement of coins, and

00:08:10.009 --> 00:08:11.970
why its ultimately doomed.

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:17.400
From our view here, it’s just one more way
to color the cube, but let’s take a moment

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:19.099
to walk through some of the corners.

00:08:19.099 --> 00:08:24.630
Let’s say you walk in and all three coins
are tails, so it’s like you’re starting

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:26.430
at the corner (0,0,0).

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:32.640
Flipping coin 0 doesn’t change the sum,
so it takes you to another red corner.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:40.120
Flipping coin 1 increases the sum to 1, so
that takes you to a green corner.

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:44.760
And flipping coin 2 takes you up to 2, bringing
you to a blue corner.

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:49.899
The fact that you have access to whatever
color you want is a reflection of the fact

00:08:49.899 --> 00:08:55.050
that this strategy will always win if that’s
the corner you start on.

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:59.940
On the other hand, let’s say you started
at (0,1,0).

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:07.470
Flipping coin 0 takes you to another green
corner, but flipping either coin 1 or coin

00:09:07.470 --> 00:09:14.850
2 will take you to a red corner, and there’s
no way to get to a blue corner.

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:20.930
Basically what’s happening here is that
you have the options to subtract 1, or add

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:40.260
2, and working mod 3 those are actually the
same operation.

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:46.230
But even without thinking about sums mod 3
or anything like that, you can see this in

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:51.610
our picture manifested as a corner with two
neighbors of the same color.

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:55.690
If you don’t have a birds eye view of all
possible strategies, when you find that any

00:09:55.690 --> 00:09:59.780
specific one that you try doesn’t work,
you’re kind just left to wonder if maybe

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:03.470
there’s some sneaky clever trick you just
haven’t thought of.

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:07.779
But thinking of colors on the cube, you’re
naturally led to an interesting combinatorial

00:10:07.779 --> 00:10:14.399
question: Is there a way to color the cube
so that the three neighbors of any given vertex

00:10:14.399 --> 00:10:19.690
will always represent red, green and blue.

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:23.680
Maybe it seems bizarre, even convoluted to
go from a puzzle with chess boards and coins

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:27.630
to talking about painting the corners of a
cube, but this is actually a much more natural

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:29.110
step than you might expect.

00:10:29.110 --> 00:10:33.341
I’ve talked with a lot of people about this
puzzle, and what I love is that many of the

00:10:33.341 --> 00:10:38.620
experienced problem solvers immediately jump,
unprompted, to talking about coloring corners

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:43.260
of a cube, as if it’s a kind of de facto
natural language for this puzzle.

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:44.490
And it really is.

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:48.620
Thinking about binary strings as vertices
of a high dimensional cube, with bit flips

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:53.389
corresponding to edges, actually comes up
a lot, especially in coding theory, like the

00:10:53.389 --> 00:10:55.790
error correction stuff I referenced earlier.

00:10:55.790 --> 00:11:00.750
What’s more, you very often hear mathematicians
talk about coloring things as a way to describe

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:04.060
partitioning them into different sets.

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:08.550
If you’ve ever heard of the hilariously
enormous number Graham’s constant, for example,

00:11:08.550 --> 00:11:13.120
the problem where that came up was phrased
in terms of assigning to colors to a high

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:14.820
dimensional cube.

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:19.269
Though in that case, colors were given to
pairs of vertices instead of individual ones.

00:11:19.269 --> 00:11:23.670
The point is, analyzing how to color a potentially
high dimensional cube is a more transferable

00:11:23.670 --> 00:11:25.389
skill than you might think.

00:11:25.389 --> 00:11:31.860
So to our question, can you make it so that
every vertex has a red, green and blue neighbor?

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:37.130
Remember, this is the same thing as having
an encoding for key locations so that you’re

00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:41.180
always one flip away from communicating whichever
key location you want.

00:11:41.180 --> 00:11:46.089
It would actually be enlightening if you paused
the video and tried this, it’s like a weird

00:11:46.089 --> 00:11:48.870
3-dimensional sudoku.

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:53.170
Very similar to a sudoku, in fact, in the
sense that you want certain subsets to be

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:56.420
filled with all 3 possible states.

00:11:56.420 --> 00:12:02.110
You might start painting one corner an arbitrary
color, say red, then painting its three neighbors

00:12:02.110 --> 00:12:04.730
red, green and blue.

00:12:04.730 --> 00:12:13.250
It’s red neighbor needs to be adjacent to
a green and a blue, so you can draw that in.

00:12:13.250 --> 00:12:17.579
But at least how I’ve done it here, you’re
stuck, unable to choose the right colors for

00:12:17.579 --> 00:12:19.490
the next two; can you see why?

00:12:19.490 --> 00:12:24.440
What I’d like to share is a lovely little
argument that explains not only why this can

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:28.880
never work in three dimensions, but why it
can’t work in any dimension that’s not

00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:30.560
a power of 2.

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:35.839
The idea is that the symmetry in the property
we want implies that there should be an equal

00:12:35.839 --> 00:12:38.250
number of red, green and blue vertices.

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:43.720
But that would mean 8/3 of each, which is
not possible.

00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:48.570
Before I go on, pause to see if you can think
of a way to solidify that intuition; it’s

00:12:48.570 --> 00:12:57.730
a fun exercise in turning a vague instinct
into a solid proof.

00:12:57.730 --> 00:13:02.350
One way is to imagine a process where you
go through each corner, and count how many

00:13:02.350 --> 00:13:07.019
of its neighbors are a particular color, say
red.

00:13:07.019 --> 00:13:11.560
So each step here we’re looking at the three
neighbors of a given vertex, counting up the

00:13:11.560 --> 00:13:17.540
red ones, and adding that to a total tally.

00:13:17.540 --> 00:13:23.300
For this coloring that count came out to be
12, but if we had the property we wanted,

00:13:23.300 --> 00:13:27.639
every corner would have exactly 1 red neighbor,
so you’d get 8.

00:13:27.639 --> 00:13:33.829
On the other hand, every red corner will be
counted exactly 3 times, once for each instance

00:13:33.829 --> 00:13:38.590
when it’s somebody’s neighbor, so that
final tally must be 3 times the total number

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:40.400
of red corners.

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:44.320
So it’s simple, just find a coloring where
8/3’s of the corners are red!

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:46.240
Isn’t that nice?

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:50.100
Counting how many times some corner has a
red neighbor is the same as counting how many

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:55.620
times a red corner has some neighbor, and
that’s enough to get us a contradiction.

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:59.550
What’s also nice about this argument is
that it immediately generalizes to higher

00:13:59.550 --> 00:14:01.060
dimensions.

00:14:01.060 --> 00:14:06.630
Think about solving the chessboard puzzle
with n squares; again, the puzzle is to associate

00:14:06.630 --> 00:14:15.070
each arrangement of coins with some state,
some key location, so that the arrangements

00:14:15.070 --> 00:14:23.459
you can get to with one flip represent all
the possible states; any possible key location.

00:14:23.459 --> 00:14:28.470
Even if we can’t visualize most high dimensional
cubes we can still talk about vertices of

00:14:28.470 --> 00:14:33.740
such a cube and their neighbors as a way to
describe bit strings and those which sit one

00:14:33.740 --> 00:14:34.740
bit flip away.

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:41.160
There are really just two relevant facts:
Standing on one of these vertices, you have

00:14:41.160 --> 00:14:48.320
n different neighbors, and the total number
of vertices is 2^n, one for each bit string

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:50.990
of length n.

00:14:50.990 --> 00:14:55.690
You can now play the same game we did in 3
dimensions, going through each corner and

00:14:55.690 --> 00:14:57.900
counting its red neighbors.

00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:03.710
If it’s possible to do the coloring you
want, this sum will be 2^n, one for each vertex.

00:15:03.710 --> 00:15:09.610
On the other hand, since each red corner is
counted once for each of its neighbors, that

00:15:09.610 --> 00:15:13.570
count should be n times the total number of
red corners.

00:15:13.570 --> 00:15:19.250
Since the left-hand side is a power of 2,
the right hand side needs to be a power of

00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:25.540
2, which can only ever work if n itself is
some smaller power of 2.

00:15:25.540 --> 00:15:31.800
For example in four dimensions, or 64 dimensions,
there is no contradiction; it’s at least

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:35.060
possible to evenly divide the vertices among
the colors.

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:40.500
To be clear, that’s not the same as saying
there necessarily is a solution for powers

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:44.529
of two, just that it can’t be ruled out
yet.

00:15:44.529 --> 00:15:48.260
This, to me, is completely delightful.

00:15:48.260 --> 00:15:52.720
Just by imagining coloring the corners of
a cube and counting how many are of each color,

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:57.279
you can conclude that no possible strategy,
no matter how clever you are, can work in

00:15:57.279 --> 00:16:03.040
all cases for this chessboard puzzle if the
number of squares is not a power of 2.

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:10.560
So if we knocked off a square, or if the board
was 6x6 instead of 8x8, the task is hopeless.

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:14.959
It also means that the solution to the chessboard
puzzle, which I’ll point you to in a moment,

00:16:14.959 --> 00:16:19.480
can be viewed as a particularly symmetric
way to color the corners of a high dimensional

00:16:19.480 --> 00:16:23.430
cube in a manner disallowed in most dimensions.

00:16:23.430 --> 00:16:28.620
If you’re curious, I couldn’t resist showing
this explicitly for a 4-dimensional cube.

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:33.180
In the same way that you can take a 3d cube
and project it down into two-dimensions with

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:38.000
a little perspective to show the same graph
structure of vertices and edges on a flat

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:44.660
plane, we can do the same projecting a 4d
cube into 3d space and still get a full view

00:16:44.660 --> 00:16:48.639
of how all the vertices and edges are connected.

00:16:48.639 --> 00:16:53.089
If you wanted to try your hand at a sort of
4-dimensional cousin of a sudoku, you could

00:16:53.089 --> 00:16:59.279
pause and see if you can figure out how to
color these vertices in such a way that the

00:16:59.279 --> 00:17:05.860
four neighbors of any vertex represent all
four colors.

00:17:05.860 --> 00:17:10.000
Using essentially the same computation that
solves the chessboard puzzle for the four-square

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:17.510
case, I can get the computer to draw it out
for us.

00:17:17.510 --> 00:17:28.250
At this point, I’d like you to hop over
to StandupMaths where Matt and I show how

00:17:28.250 --> 00:17:29.450
this solution works.

